Subject: webrtc: offer only the address families the proxy's relay can carry

Patch 0086 gave WebRTC's UDP sockets a SOCKS5 UDP association so that the
address a page is told is the address its packets come from. It decided per
socket, after the socket had been asked for, and refused the ones the proxy
could not carry. That was right about the packets and wrong about everything
else, and the measurement says so.

Through a residential SOCKS5 exit whose host has an A record and no AAAA
record, one peer connection produced two wildcard UDP ports. The IPv4 one
gathered its mDNS host candidate and its server-reflexive candidate and logged
`Port completed gathering candidates.`. The IPv6 one appears exactly twice in a
`--v=1` log -- `Port created` and `Added port to allocator` -- and never again:
no `Port ready.`, no `Gathered candidate`, no completion. Its socket had been
refused with -109 `ERR_ADDRESS_UNREACHABLE` inside
`SOCKS5DatagramServerSocket::DoResolveProxyComplete`, before a byte reached the
proxy, because `Listen()` had recorded the requested family and the resolver
answer held no address in it.

`iceGatheringState` was therefore still `gathering` after 50 s, and the null
end-of-candidates candidate never fired. The two IPv4 candidates were correct
and complete the whole time. So the candidate COUNT was never the defect -- one
mDNS host plus one IPv4 srflx is the right shape for an IPv4-only egress, and
this exit is IPv4-only three times over: no AAAA record on the proxy host, only
`ATYP=0x01` destinations forwarded by the relay (an IPv6 destination, a
v4-mapped destination and a domain destination each got no answer over repeated
tries, while the IPv4 destination answered), and `ERR_SOCKS_CONNECTION_FAILED`
for IPv6-only hostnames over TCP as well. The defect was the port that never
finished.

That is the worst shape in this area. A detector that reads candidates at its
own timeout prints a truncated list. A detector that waits for `complete`, or
for `a=end-of-candidates`, waits for ever. `--fingerprint-webrtc-udp=block` was
worse still: zero candidates, `c=IN IP4 0.0.0.0`, and gathering that never
finished, with no proxy configured at all. Stock Chrome reaches the
zero-candidate SDP under the `WebRTCIPHandling=disable_non_proxied_udp`
enterprise policy -- but it reaches it with gathering COMPLETE. The
permanently-gathering variant is reachable in no stock configuration.

The reason a per-socket refusal cannot be made to work is structural. A relayed
socket has no address family of its own. The only address it ever sends to is
its association's relay endpoint; the ICE candidate it produces is that endpoint
verbatim; and the destination of a relayed datagram lives in the RFC 1928
per-datagram header, independent of the local socket's family. So the local bind
family, the transport family and the candidate family are all the family of the
PROXY's relay endpoint, and no second local family is ever needed. Which
families WebRTC may use is a property of the configured proxy, and it has to be
answered before a port exists, because a port cannot be taken back once
created.

Four places change, one per layer.

`P2PSocketManager` turns the egress decision into a family pair.
`ResolveUdpEgress` already answered direct, relay-through-SOCKS5 or blocked; the
manager now caches that answer and derives from it which families it covers:
both for direct, neither for blocked, and for a relay the families the proxy
host resolves in. That last one is the same lookup each
`SOCKS5DatagramServerSocket` performs for itself, hoisted so the answer exists
before any port does, with the same unnarrowed query type for the same reason --
a query for one family would make the media plane's lookup distinguishable at
the resolver. The pair rides the existing
`P2PNetworkNotificationClient::NetworkListChanged` message, which gains two
booleans; no new interface, no new message, nothing a page can read. It is
re-read on each network-change edge rather than once for the manager's life,
because a network change is also when a real machine's candidate set changes,
and taking the list and the families from one snapshot is what keeps the socket
a request gets from contradicting what the renderer was told.

`IpcNetworkManager` drops interfaces of a family that has no egress, so
`MergeNetworkList` never offers one and `BasicPortAllocatorSession::GetNetworks`
cannot hand one back. When NO family has an egress the list is left alone: "no
UDP at all" is a decision for the allocator's flags, and dropping every
interface would take TURN over TCP and TLS with it.

`PeerConnectionDependencyFactory::CreatePortAllocator` sets
`enable_nonproxied_udp = false` when no family has an egress, which raises
`PORTALLOCATOR_DISABLE_UDP | PORTALLOCATOR_DISABLE_STUN |
PORTALLOCATOR_DISABLE_UDP_RELAY` and reaches exactly the state stock reaches
under `disable_non_proxied_udp`: no UDP, STUN or UDP-relay port is created,
gathering completes with no UDP candidate, and TCP and TLS TURN still work.

`IpcPacketSocketFactory::CreateUdpSocket` returns nullptr for a family with no
egress. This is the one that carries the guarantee, and it is not redundant with
the two above. The families arrive with the network list, so the first peer
connection in a renderer is constructed before the answer exists -- the
allocator's flags are fixed at construction while `CreateUdpSocket` is called at
allocation time, after the list has arrived, which is also why the flag can
default to "UDP allowed" without ever being wrong in the direction that leaks.
It is also the only choke point every UDP socket passes through, including the
shared allocation-sequence socket and a TURN/UDP port's own. Returning nullptr
is the answer webrtc already handles on every port type: the sequence makes no
UDP or STUN port, its relay and TCP phases still run, and gathering completes.

Two smaller changes make the above hold in cases the family pair cannot predict.

`SOCKS5DatagramServerSocket` no longer treats the requested family as a
requirement. `DoResolveProxyComplete` prefers an endpoint in the family
`Listen()` bound -- so a proxy reachable in both families keeps one association
per family and each family's host candidate stays in its own family -- and falls
back to whatever the proxy does resolve in, then rebinds the datagram socket to
the wildcard of that family. Closing and reopening is the only way to change a
bound socket's family and it costs nothing here: nothing has been sent or
received, the port is ephemeral and discarded anyway, and `GetLocalAddress()`
reports the relay's address rather than this socket's, so no address the caller
has seen changes. The buffer sizes the caller set are replayed, because a
reopened socket loses them. `ResolveRelayEndpoint`'s existing
`reported.size() != proxy_address.size()` substitution rule then guarantees
`relay_endpoint_` shares the bound family, so `GetLocalAddress`, the
`SocketCreated` address and the host candidate all agree. `ERR_ADDRESS_UNREACHABLE`
is gone; the only remaining resolution failure is a proxy with no address at
all, which is `ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED`.

And `webrtc::UDPPort` subscribes to its socket's close event. This is an upstream
bug fix, not a fingerprint change, and it is what makes the completion promise
unconditional. `UDPPort::Init` subscribed to sent-packet, ready-to-send and
address-ready and to nothing that fires when the socket dies;
`IpcPacketSocket::OnError` sets its state to error and calls `NotifyClosed` with
nobody listening; `PrepareAddress` reports an address only for a socket that is
already `STATE_BOUND`. So any socket that failed before `OnOpen` left `ready_`
false, `MaybeSetPortCompleteOrError` unreached, and the session waiting on a port
that would never finish -- a plain `bind()` failure does it too, and 0086 turned
it into the normal case. `AsyncPacketSocket` already has the subscription point;
`OnSocketClosed` uses it, reports a port error for a socket that never bound, and
ignores a close after the port has reported its address, which is ordinary
teardown. `PrepareAddress` gains the matching branch for the socket that was
already dead when the port asked, which a shared allocation-sequence socket can
be: the sequence creates it one task before it creates the port, so its close
event can fire before anything is subscribed, and waiting for a second one would
wait for ever. Together these cover the failures no family pair can foresee: an
ASSOCIATE answered `REP 0x07` after the socket exists, a control connection that
drops mid-handshake, a proxy that stops answering.

What a page sees, per configuration:

  - no proxy, or `--fingerprint-webrtc-udp=direct`: both families, unchanged.
    The measured direct shape is 2 mDNS host candidates and 2 srflx literals,
    one per family, gathering complete in about a second, and Apostate already
    matched stock Chrome field for field there.

  - a proxy that resolves in one family: that family only. One mDNS host
    candidate and one srflx literal, gathering complete -- indistinguishable
    from an IPv4-only desktop, and the same shape the broken build printed
    except that it now finishes.

  - a proxy that resolves in both: the full four.

  - no UDP egress, from `block` or from a proxy that cannot relay a datagram:
    zero UDP candidates and gathering COMPLETE, with `c=IN IP4 0.0.0.0` -- which
    is what stock produces under `disable_non_proxied_udp` with no TURN server.

  - any of the above where the association fails after the socket exists: the
    port errors and gathering completes.

Also corrected: 0086's comment on `P2PSocketUdp::OnEgressReady` claimed one
refusal "per interface the proxy cannot carry -- six on a dual-stack host". The
measured number with media permission denied is one, because only two wildcard
UDP ports exist. The comment now says one, and says what a refusal means now
that the renderer is told the families in advance: the egress failed after the
socket was requested, not that the family was never going to work.

No page-visible observable is added. The mojo message gains two booleans a page
cannot read; there is no new JavaScript property, no wrapper, no CDP override,
no injection, no new NetLog event type and no new process name. No value is
randomised per call: the family pair is a function of the configured proxy's DNS
answer, two reads of one candidate in one launch agree, and variation comes only
from the proxy the user configured. Capacity only goes down -- a family is
removed from what WebRTC is offered, never added.

What is not closed:

  - The interface list itself is still the host's. This patch decides which
    FAMILIES are offered, not which interfaces; `DoGetNetworkList` still
    enumerates the real ones and the renderer still receives them, so candidate
    priority, `network-id` and `network-cost` still describe this machine.
    That is the `webrtc.ice-candidates` row and a separate patch.

  - The family pair is the families the proxy's NAME resolves in, not the
    families in which an ASSOCIATE actually succeeds. A proxy that resolves in
    both and relays only IPv4 destinations answers ASSOCIATE in both, so both
    families produce a host candidate and the IPv6 one gets no server-reflexive
    partner because its STUN request is dropped on the way out. That is what a
    machine with IPv6 configured and no IPv6 route looks like. Closing it needs
    an ASSOCIATE probe per family before any candidate is offered, which costs
    three round trips to the proxy on every network change.

  - `WebRtcUdpPortRange` still does not apply to a relayed socket, for 0086's
    reason: the port a page sees is the proxy's.

Compile-verified: `scripts/checkfile.sh` on out/macos-arm64 for all ten changed
translation units -- socks5_datagram_server_socket.cc, socket_manager.cc,
socket_udp.cc, socket_test_utils.cc, socket_dispatcher.cc,
ipc_network_manager.cc, ipc_network_manager_test.cc, ipc_socket_factory.cc,
peer_connection_dependency_factory.cc and stun_port.cc.

Behaviour-unverified: no linked browser has run this, because the next build has
not happened. `/tmp/nr/drive.js <label> <binary> [args...]` is the CDP gatherer
that produced every measurement quoted above; it writes the candidate list, the
SDP, the gathering state and whether the null candidate fired. The three runs
that confirm this patch, and what each must print:

  1. `--fingerprint-platform=windows --fingerprint=424242 --proxy-server=socks5://<creds>@proxy.geonode.io:12000`
     -> exactly two candidates: one `typ host` at a `.local` name, one
     `typ udp ... typ srflx` at the exit's IPv4 address; `gatheringState`
     `complete` and the null candidate fired, within about 5 s. Before this
     patch: the same two candidates, `gathering`, no null candidate, after 50 s.

  2. `--fingerprint-platform=windows --fingerprint-webrtc-udp=block`
     -> zero candidates AND `gatheringState` `complete` with the null candidate
     fired. Before this patch: zero candidates, `gathering`, after 25 s.

  3. `--fingerprint-platform=windows --fingerprint=424242` with no proxy
     -> the unchanged four: 2 mDNS host, 2 srflx, one pair per family,
     `complete`.

